    "body": "The section \"Dealing with Infinity in Nonce Aggregation\" sketches a reduction from the unforgeability of the modified scheme, which sets the final nonce R to G whenever it would be the point at infinity, to the unforgeability of the original MuSig2* scheme. The sketch let the reduction translate aggnonce' = bytes(33, 0) || bytes(33, 0) into aggnonce = cbytes_ext(G) || bytes(33, 0). But the nonce coefficient b is derived by hashing the aggregate nonce, so the adversary expects a b computed from aggnonce' while the original game computes b from aggnonce. The resulting partial signatures do not match, and the sketch does not work as a black-box reduction.\n\nReplace the sketch with a reduction that programs the random oracles. For each pair of aggregate public key and message, it maps the three aggregate nonces whose final nonce is G or -G in the modified scheme, namely bytes(33, 0) || bytes(33, 0), cbytes(G) || bytes(33, 0), and cbytes(-G) || bytes(33, 0), to three aggregate nonces of its own choice whose final nonce in the original game is a random W or -W, and it swaps xbytes(G) with xbytes(W) in the inputs of the challenge hash. The three values that the partial signature depends on, i.e., the nonce coefficient, the challenge, and the parity of the final nonce, then agree between the adversary's view and the original game. Since the reduction knows the discrete logarithms of all group elements it introduces, the argument also applies in the algebraic group model, in which the security proof of MuSig2* is given. The full argument is in a footnote. Bump the version to 1.0.5.\n\nThe problem with the previous sketch was found by mllwchrry while reviewing the corresponding section of the FROST signing BIP draft, see https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing/issues/73.\n\nThe reduction has been found and written down with the help of Claude Fable 5.1.",
