      "message": "BIP-327: Fix reduction sketch for infinity in nonce aggregation\n\nThe section \"Dealing with Infinity in Nonce Aggregation\" sketches a\nreduction from the unforgeability of the modified scheme, which sets the\nfinal nonce R to G whenever it would be the point at infinity, to the\nunforgeability of the original MuSig2* scheme. The sketch let the\nreduction translate aggnonce' = bytes(33, 0) || bytes(33, 0) into\naggnonce = cbytes_ext(G) || bytes(33, 0). But the nonce coefficient b is\nderived by hashing the aggregate nonce, so the adversary expects a b\ncomputed from aggnonce' while the original game computes b from\naggnonce. The resulting partial signatures do not match, and the sketch\ndoes not work as a black-box reduction.\n\nReplace the sketch with a reduction that programs the random oracles.\nFor each pair of aggregate public key and message, it maps the three\naggregate nonces whose final nonce is G or -G in the modified scheme,\nnamely bytes(33, 0) || bytes(33, 0), cbytes(G) || bytes(33, 0), and\ncbytes(-G) || bytes(33, 0), to three aggregate nonces of its own choice\nwhose final nonce in the original game is a random W or -W, and it swaps\nxbytes(G) with xbytes(W) in the inputs of the challenge hash. The three\nvalues that the partial signature depends on, i.e., the nonce\ncoefficient, the challenge, and the parity of the final nonce, then\nagree between the adversary's view and the original game. Since the\nreduction knows the discrete logarithms of all group elements it\nintroduces, the argument also applies in the algebraic group model, in\nwhich the security proof of MuSig2* is given. The full argument is in a\nfootnote. Bump the version to 1.0.5.\n\nThe problem with the previous sketch was found by mllwchrry while\nreviewing the corresponding section of the FROST signing BIP draft, see\nhttps://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing/issues/73.\n\nThe reduction has been found and written down with the help of Claude\nFable 5.1.",
